      "cephalopod": "🐙 Cephalopod",
      "cephalopod_arms_can_regenerate_if_seve": "Cephalopod arms can REGENERATE if severed. Full regeneration takes 4-6 weeks + restores both sensory + motor function. Imperadore is leading research on the cellular mechanisms.",
      "cephalopod_axons_are_1_mm_in_diameter_": "Cephalopod axons are ~1 mm in diameter — over 1000x larger than mammalian neurons. This is why Hodgkin + Huxley used them for the 1952 Nobel-Prize-winning work.",
      "cephalopod_beak_is_among_the_hardest_b": "Cephalopod beak is among the hardest biological materials known. It has a gradient stiffness — soft at the base + extremely hard at the cutting edge — that engineers are studying for biomaterial design.",
      "cephalopod_behaviour_2nd_ed": "Cephalopod Behaviour (2nd ed.)",
      "cephalopod_behaviour_2nd_ed_2": "Cephalopod Behaviour (2nd ed.)",
      "cephalopod_behaviour_blog": "Cephalopod Behaviour blog",
      "cephalopod_biology_connects_to_nearly_": "Cephalopod biology connects to nearly every other school subject. The Cross-disciplinary tab maps these connections. Some that work well:\n\nMath: stats analysis of trial data (Sample Data Sets). Population modeling.\n\nELA: Sy Montgomery + Godfrey-Smith books. Reading scientific abstracts. Position papers.\n\nSocial studies: history of cephalopod cultural depictions. Welfare legislation across countries.\n\nArt: Hokusai. Iridophore patterns. Field-note illustration.\n\nComputer science: simulation design. Distributed cognition algorithms.\n\nEngineering: biomimicry (soft robotics, suction adhesives).\n\nHealth: tetrodotoxin medications. Squid axon in cardiology + neurology.\n\nEthics: sentience recognition + animal welfare.",
      "cephalopod_biology_has_been_featured_i": "Cephalopod biology has been featured in popular science books, documentaries, fictional novels, art exhibits, and high-end menus. Their cultural footprint expanded dramatically after \"My Octopus Teacher\" (2020).",
      "cephalopod_biology_is_a_small_collabor": "Cephalopod biology is a small, collaborative field. Most active researchers number in the hundreds globally. The community spans university labs, marine stations (Woods Hole MBL, MBARI, AZTI in Spain), aquariums, and museum curators. Many of these scientists have explicitly written about animal welfare, citizen science, and increasing field accessibility — the kind of values that aligned with this tool's UDL design.",
      "cephalopod_biology_is_among_the_riches": "Cephalopod biology is among the richest entry points into modern biology, ecology, evolution, ethics, and cognition. They're ALIEN ENOUGH to provoke curiosity (no bones, blue blood, color-blind color-matching, distributed intelligence) but FAMILIAR ENOUGH to engage emotional connection (intelligence, individual personality, sentience). They're short-lived but their populations are growing under climate change — making them BOTH a curiosity AND a real ecological story. The science is genuinely OPEN — questions remain, debates are active, and student curiosity can engage with REAL unresolved problems. We are not just teaching facts; we are inviting students into a working scientific community.\n\nFor middle school: cephalopod camouflage is a perfect entry into adaptive evolution. The \"color-blind animal that matches color\" puzzle is a real biological mystery that students can engage with.\n\nFor high school + AP Bio: cephalopods raise convergent evolution, RNA editing, semelparity, ethics, climate science, and biomedicine in a single integrated package.\n\nFor elementary: cephalopods are EMOTIONAL — students bond with animals they meet through the simulator. This builds long-term engagement with science as a whole.",
      "cephalopod_biology_is_one_of_the_few_s": "Cephalopod biology is one of the few scientific fields where amateur observations regularly contribute to peer-reviewed papers. New species are still being discovered. Behavior is poorly documented for most species. ",
      "cephalopod_biology_research_has_produc": "Cephalopod biology research has produced over 5000 peer-reviewed publications between 2015 + 2024. The field is RAPIDLY growing — driven by genomics + welfare science + popular interest.",
      "cephalopod_biomass_density_is_highest_": "Cephalopod biomass density is highest at boundaries between habitats (reef-sand edges, kelp-rock edges) where prey + shelter both occur.",
      "cephalopod_blood_does_not_clot_like_ve": "Cephalopod blood does NOT clot like vertebrate blood. Instead, hemocytes seal wounds via a different mechanism.",
      "cephalopod_blue_blood_was_first_identi": "Cephalopod blue blood was first identified in 1851 by French biologist Alfred Frédéricq — though hemocyanin's copper basis wasn't mapped until the 20th century.",
      "cephalopod_brain_vs_mammal_brain": "Cephalopod Brain vs. Mammal Brain",
      "cephalopod_camo_strategy": "🎨 Cephalopod camo strategy: ",
      "cephalopod_camouflage_works_against_po": "Cephalopod camouflage works against POLARIZATION-sensitive predators (like other cephalopods) via polarized-light skin elements — a hidden visual channel humans don't see.",
      "cephalopod_cannibalism_is_the_norm_lar": "Cephalopod cannibalism is the norm. Larger common octopus eats smaller common octopus. Humboldt squid pack-hunt smaller squid. Same camouflage tricks don't work — your predator is using them too.",
      "cephalopod_chapter_alongside_other_ani": "Cephalopod chapter alongside other animals. Photo-style cut-paper illustrations.",
      "cephalopod_chromatophore_driven_body_p": "Cephalopod chromatophore-driven body patterns are so complex that they're being studied as a model for understanding INFORMATION CODING in biological systems.",
      "cephalopod_chromatophores_are_organize": "Cephalopod chromatophores are organized in distinct LAYERS — yellow-orange (superficial), red (middle), brown-black (deep) — allowing complex pattern combinations.",
      "cephalopod_cognition": "Cephalopod Cognition",
