      "4_read_where_the": "4️⃣ Read where the ",
      "50_means_50_out_of_100_build_a_100_fla": "50% means 50 out of 100. Build a 100-flat. Show 50%: half the flat = 50 ones. Show 25%: 1/4 of the flat = 25 ones. Show 75%, 10%, 5%.",
      "5_2": "+ 5",
      "5_nickels_4_nickels_5_pennies_3_nickel": "5 nickels; 4 nickels + 5 pennies; 3 nickels + 10 pennies; 2 nickels + 15 pennies; 1 nickel + 20 pennies; 25 pennies. (Six possible combinations.)",
      "7_bikes_would_have_14_wheels_4_short_e": "7 bikes would have 14 wheels — 4 short. Each swap (bike→trike) adds 1 wheel.",
      "a_10x10_grid_of_1_100_click_to_highlig": "A 10x10 grid of 1-100. Click to highlight cells. Skip-count overlays reveal multiplication patterns. CCSS 2.NBT.A.2: skip-counting by 5s, 10s, 100s.",
      "a_4th_grader_who_still_needs_blocks_fo": "A 4th grader who still NEEDS blocks for 23 + 47 has a problem. The blocks were a scaffold; if they never come off, fluency never develops. Move to representational (pictures) by grade 2-3, to abstract (symbols) by grade 3-4. Re-enter concrete only for new concepts.",
      "a_5_by_2_grid_fill_cells_to_show_a_num": "A 5-by-2 grid. Fill cells to show a number. Subitizing (instant recognition without counting) develops here. CCSS K.OA.A.4: the \"what makes 10?\" question is the gateway to all later arithmetic fluency.",
      "a_6th_grader_who_needs_blocks_for_23_4": "A 6th grader who needs blocks for 23+47 has not been failed by the blocks — they were never taken away soon enough.",
      "a_baboon_fibula_with_29_notches_likely": "A baboon fibula with 29 notches — likely the oldest known mathematical artifact. Possibly a lunar phase tally.",
      "a_baboon_fibula_with_three_columns_of_": "A baboon fibula with three columns of grouped notches. Some columns are pairs of doubles; one column contains only prime numbers.",
      "a_built_construction_is_a_powerful_art": "A built construction is a powerful artifact — leave it on the table while the class talks about it. Save it to the Library if you need it back tomorrow. Don't make students dismantle a hard-won build to \"be ready for the next subject.\"",
      "a_challenge_bank_a_ccss_standards_brow": "a challenge bank, a CCSS standards browser, a printable activity-card library, and ",
      "a_k_5_progression_chart_showing_which_": "A K-5 progression chart showing which manipulatives are typically introduced at which grade and which standards they support. Click any cell to open that tool.",
      "a_lands_on_multiples_of_3_b_lands_on_m": "A lands on multiples of 3. B lands on multiples of 5. They meet on common multiples.",
      "a_number_bond_with_whole_12_and_parts_": "A number bond with whole 12 and parts 5 and 7 gives 4 facts: 5+7=12, 7+5=12, 12−5=7, 12−7=5. Have students list all 4 from any single bond.",
      "a_student_who_shows_8_5_as_9_4_because": "A student who shows 8 + 5 as \"9 + 4\" (because they took 1 from 5 and added it to 8 first) has invented a make-ten strategy. Surface it; name it; teach the class to try it. The textbook approach is not the only correct approach.",
      "a_table_covered_in_4_different_manipul": "A table covered in 4 different manipulatives is a recipe for distraction, not differentiation. Pick ONE per lesson. The other 11 in this tool are great — for other lessons.",
