      "use_the_double_ten_frame_to_add_within": "Use the double ten frame to add within 20.",
      "use_the_glossary_to_introduce_1_2_voca": "Use the Glossary to introduce 1–2 vocab terms per session.",
      "use_the_hexagon_as_the_unit_whole_buil": "Use the hexagon as the unit whole; build other shapes as fractions of it.",
      "use_the_show_primes_button_on_the_hund": "Use the \"Show primes\" button on the Hundreds Chart. Count them. Notice the gaps. Discuss: which row has the most primes? Which has the fewest?",
      "using_too_many_manipulatives_at_once": "Using too many manipulatives at once",
      "virtual_manipulatives": "Virtual Manipulatives",
      "vocabulary_is_the_curriculum_a_student": "Vocabulary IS the curriculum. A student who hears \"regroup,\" \"compose,\" \"decompose,\" \"fluently,\" and \"place value\" enough times in context will internalize the system. Definitions are tied to the specific manipulative that demonstrates each.",
      "walk_through_cra_for_4_3_4_groups_of_3": "Walk through CRA for 4 × 3: 4 groups of 3 white rods, then a 4-by-3 array of dots, then the equation 4 × 3 = 12.",
      "web_based_tools_like_this_one_let_stud": "Web-based tools like this one let students interact with classical manipulatives anywhere. The pedagogy is the same; only the medium has changed.",
      "what_does_productive_struggle_look_lik": "What does \"productive struggle\" look like vs \"frustration\"? How would you tell the difference in a classroom?",
      "what_integer_multiplied_by_itself_equa": "What integer, multiplied by itself, equals 49?",
      "what_is_4_3_has_one_answer_convergent_": "\"What is 4 + 3?\" has one answer (convergent). \"Show me 7 in three different ways\" has many (divergent). Divergent prompts expose how students think — which is the assessment that matters.",
      "what_is_each_digit_worth": "What is each digit worth?",
      "what_s_the_missing_part": "What's the missing part?",
      "when_do_we_meet": "When do we meet?",
      "white_1_red_2_light_green_3_purple_4_y": "White=1, Red=2, Light green=3, Purple=4, Yellow=5, Dark green=6, Black=7, Brown=8, Blue=9, Orange=10. ",
      "whole_5_drag_the_slider_so_part_1_rang": "Whole = 5. Drag the slider so part 1 ranges through 0..5. Each time, say the bond aloud: \"5 is 0 and 5, 5 is 1 and 4, ...\"",
      "why_cra": "Why CRA?",
      "why_does_the_same_task_feel_different_": "Why does the same task feel different in K vs 5th grade vs 8th grade?",
      "why_vocabulary_matters_a_marzano_2004_": "💡 Why vocabulary matters: a Marzano (2004) meta-analysis estimated that direct vocabulary instruction in a content area produces an average percentile gain of ~33 points. ",
      "william_oughtred_slides_two_log_scales": "William Oughtred slides two log scales past each other. The slide rule becomes the calculator for the next 350 years.",
      "workspace_expression": "Workspace — expression: ",
      "worst_case_you_grab_one_of_each_color_": "Worst case: you grab one of each color first. The next one must match SOMETHING.",
      "x_square": "+x² (square)",
      "x_square_dashed": "−x² (square, dashed)",
      "yellow": "+ Yellow",
      "yellow_1_red_1_a_yellow_red_pair_0_a_z": "Yellow = +1; red = -1. A yellow + red pair = 0 (a \"zero pair\"). The most concrete model for understanding negative numbers and integer arithmetic. CCSS 6.NS.C.5, 7.NS.A.1.",
      "you_have_just_translated_a_real_physic": "You have just translated a real physical idea into a symbol. That symbol now MEANS that thing.",
      "you_re_summing_1_2_3_10_use_the_trick_": "You're summing 1+2+3+...+10. Use the trick: pair (1+10)+(2+9)+(3+8)+(4+7)+(5+6) = ?",
      "you_will_walk_through_the_same_problem": "You will walk through the same problem in three forms: physical objects → a picture → a written equation.",
      "your_hypothesis_which_slider_should_a_": "Your hypothesis (which slider should a beginning teacher worry about most? Why?)",
