      "a_2300_year_journey_from_aristarchus_t": "A 2300-year journey from Aristarchus to JWST. Each discovery built on the last. Science is a relay race — every astronomer stands on the shoulders of those before them.",
      "a_au": "a (AU)",
      "a_biosignature_is_any_evidence_of_past": "A biosignature is any evidence of past or present life. Scientists look for: (1) liquid water, (2) organic molecules, (3) atmospheric gases like oxygen or methane that shouldn’t exist without life producing them, (4) energy sources. The best candidates in our solar system: Mars (ancient water), Europa (subsurface ocean), Enceladus (water geysers), and Titan (methane lakes).",
      "a_cme_blasts_billions_of_tons_of_plasm": "A CME blasts billions of tons of plasma from Sun. Days later, it can cause spectacular auroras + GPS failures.",
      "a_comet_gets_two_tails_when_it_nears_t": "A comet gets two tails when it nears the Sun. Adjust distance + wind angle to see how they form.",
      "a_coronal_mass_ejection_blasts_billion": "A Coronal Mass Ejection blasts billions of tons of plasma into space at 1-2 million mph. Takes 1-5 days to reach Earth. Strong CMEs cause geomagnetic storms, vivid auroras, and can disrupt satellites + power grids.",
      "a_dozen_of_the_world_s_most_powerful_t": "A dozen of the world's most powerful telescopes. Aperture (mm) is the diameter of the primary mirror. Bigger aperture = more light + finer detail. Space telescopes avoid atmospheric distortion.",
      "a_hohmann_transfer_is_the_most_fuel_ef": "A Hohmann transfer is the most fuel-efficient way to travel between planets.",
      "a_hohmann_transfer_is_the_most_fuel_ef_2": "A Hohmann transfer is the most fuel-efficient way to move between two circular orbits. It uses two burns: one to enter the transfer ellipse, one to circularize at the destination. Δv is the total change in velocity required.",
      "a_line_from_planet_to_sun_sweeps_equal": "A line from planet to Sun sweeps equal areas in equal times — meaning planets move FASTER near the Sun (perihelion) and SLOWER far away (aphelion).",
      "a_living_reef_system_teeming_with_biod": "A living reef system teeming with biodiversity.",
      "a_magnetic_field_deflects_solar_wind_p": "A magnetic field deflects solar wind + protects atmosphere. Mars lost both; that's why it's barren.",
      "a_massive_storm_system_similar_to_jupi": "A massive storm system similar to Jupiter’s Great Red Spot.",
      "a_persistent_hexagonal_cloud_pattern_a": "A persistent hexagonal cloud pattern at Saturn’s north pole.",
      "a_planet_s_gravity_makes_its_star_wobb": "A planet's gravity makes its star wobble. We see the wobble as Doppler shift in starlight.",
      "a_planet_s_magnetic_field_creates_a_ma": "A planet’s magnetic field creates a \"magnetosphere\" — an invisible shield that deflects the solar wind (charged particles from the Sun). Earth’s magnetosphere protects our atmosphere and makes life possible. Mars lost its magnetic field 4 billion years ago, and its atmosphere was gradually stripped away. Jupiter’s magnetosphere is 20,000x stronger than Earth’s!",
      "a_planet_sweeps_equal_areas_in_equal_t": "② A planet sweeps equal areas in equal times (faster when closer to Sun)",
      "a_pulsar_is_a_rapidly_rotating_neutron": "A pulsar is a rapidly rotating neutron star. Its magnetic poles emit beams of radiation. We see a pulse only when the beam sweeps past us — like a lighthouse. Some pulse 700 times per second. Jocelyn Bell Burnell discovered the first in 1967.",
      "a_relay_beacon_is_35m_away_at_heading_": "A relay beacon is 35m away at heading 045° (NE). Calculate and navigate to its position.",
      "a_sunken_vessel_colonized_by_marine_li": "A sunken vessel colonized by marine life — an artificial reef.",
      "a_transit_happens_when_mercury_or_venu": "A transit happens when Mercury (or Venus) passes between Earth + the Sun. It looks like a small black dot on the Sun. Next Mercury transit: November 13, 2032. Next Venus transit: December 2117 — won't happen again until 2125.",
