      "lincoln_viking_3350_series_miller_digi": "Lincoln Viking 3350 series, Miller Digital Infinity / Elite, Optrel Crystal 2.0, Speedglas 9100XXi. ~$200-500.",
      "lists_all_variables_whether_each_is_es": "Lists ALL variables + whether each is essential, supplementary essential, or nonessential.",
      "lists_each_revision_with_date_descript": "Lists each revision with date + description + initials. Compare to your drawing — if the part you're holding doesn't match the print revision, STOP.",
      "lists_every_part_by_number_quantity_ma": "Lists every part by number + quantity + material spec. Cross-reference part labels in the drawing.",
      "live_bead_simulator_drag_your_arc": "Live bead simulator — drag your arc",
      "loading_3d_scene": "Loading 3D scene…",
      "loads_unloads_parts_monitors_cell_oper": "Loads/unloads parts, monitors cell operation, performs minor adjustments + maintenance. Entry-level. Often a starting point.",
      "local_exhaust_ventilation": "Local exhaust ventilation",
      "long_term_fume_exposure_bronchitis_ast": "Long-term fume exposure → bronchitis, asthma, increased pneumonia risk.",
      "look_at_a_finished_bead_click_the_defe": "Look at a finished bead. Click the defects you can spot. Each defect (porosity, undercut, lack of fusion, cold lap, crater crack, overlap) has a hover card explaining what causes it and how to fix it. Same skill that AWS-certified welding inspectors use.",
      "low_hydrogen_electrodes_e7018_stored_h": "Low-hydrogen electrodes (E7018, stored hot). Preheat to slow cooling rate (prevent martensite). Stress relief PWHT. For high-restraint welds use \"buttering\" technique (build up filler before final pass).",
      "low_melting_point_eutectics_sulfur_pho": "Low-melting-point eutectics (sulfur, phosphorus, lead contaminants; or excess sulfur in base metal) remain liquid while surrounding metal solidifies + contracts. Tensile stress pulls the still-liquid film apart.",
      "low_spatter_most_arc_energy_reaches_th": "Low spatter, most arc energy reaches the weld.",
      "low_volume_work": "Low-volume work: ",
      "lower_heat_input_per_inch": "• Lower heat input per inch",
      "lower_setup_cost_fast_deployment_for_e": "Lower setup cost. Fast deployment for emergency repair. Most accessible certification path.",
      "lower_weld_quality_class_b_by_us_navy_": "Lower weld quality (Class B by US Navy spec). Hydrogen embrittlement risk. Practical depth limit ~100 ft. Lower pay than hyperbaric.",
      "lowest_cost_per_pound_automated_only_u": "Lowest cost per pound. Automated only. Used for huge plates, ships, pipe seam welding.",
      "lowest_deposit_rate_highest_cost_per_p": "Lowest deposit rate, highest cost per pound. ONLY used where quality + appearance + corrosion resistance justify cost.",
      "lowest_deposit_rate_lots_of_waste_stub": "Lowest deposit rate. Lots of waste (stubs + flux). But low equipment cost + portable.",
      "lyman_morse_thomaston": "Lyman-Morse (Thomaston)",
      "magnetize_the_part_with_an_electromagn": "Magnetize the part with an electromagnet → apply iron particles (dry powder or wet suspension) → particles cluster at flux leakage (where field exits at defects) → defects show as colored lines.",
      "maine_has_one_of_the_densest_skilled_t": "Maine has one of the densest skilled-trades welding economies in the Northeast. Shipbuilding (Bath Iron Works alone employs ~6,500), boatbuilding (Hodgdon, Sabre, Lyman-Morse), heavy industry (paper mills, food processing), and a thick small-shop ecosystem (fabrication shops, marine repair, custom work) all need welders right now.",
      "maine_s_welding_workforce_extends_well": "Maine's welding workforce extends well beyond shipbuilding. Heavy construction, paper/pulp, food processing, road maintenance, agriculture, custom fab — all hire welders, often year-round, often with shorter training requirements.",
      "maine_s_welding_workforce_is_5_7_women": "Maine's welding workforce is ~5-7% women (slowly growing). Some shops are excellent; some still have culture work to do. Resources: Women in Welding (national organization), Maine Tradeswomen Association, AWS Women in Welding Committee. LGBTQ+ welders: Pride At Work (AFL-CIO affiliate) has resources for union members.",
      "maine_shipyards": "⚓ Maine shipyards",
      "maine_shipyards_boatbuilders_mid_coast": "⚓ Maine shipyards + boatbuilders — mid-coast cluster",
      "maine_specific": "Maine specific: ",
      "maine_specific_note": "Maine-specific note",
      "maine_welding_careers": "Maine welding careers",
      "maine_welding_ecosystem": "Maine Welding Ecosystem",
      "maine_welding_ecosystem_2": "Maine Welding Ecosystem",
      "maine_welding_employers_sample_of_wher": "Maine welding employers — sample of where welders work in-state",
      "major_brands": "🏭 Major brands",
      "major_fume_constituents_by_metal": "Major fume constituents by metal",
      "manganese_exposure": "Manganese exposure: ",
      "many_maine_ctes_region_10_mid_coast_sc": "Many Maine CTEs (Region 10, Mid-Coast School of Technology, Hancock County Technical Center) feed BIW directly. Counselors have established relationships.",
      "many_maine_welders_eventually_open_the": "Many Maine welders eventually open their own shops — sometimes after 5-15 years industry experience, sometimes immediately from CTE/EMCC. Small shop welding is hard work but it is one of the lowest-barrier paths to small business ownership.",
      "many_welders_estimated_20_are_dyslexic": "Many welders (estimated 20%+) are dyslexic, ADHD, autistic, or have other neurodivergent profiles. Welding rewards visual-spatial thinking + focused attention + tactile skill — often a great fit. Reasonable accommodations are protected by ADA. Common requests: written instructions, quieter break area, predictable schedule.",
      "marcus_t": "Marcus T.",
      "marine_fabrication_shops": "Marine + fabrication shops: ",
      "massive_setup_cost_chamber_support_shi": "Massive setup cost (chamber + support ship). Long mobilization time. Decompression schedule for diver. Niche specialty — fewer jobs.",
      "material": "Material",
      "material_process_lethal_fume_table_hot": "Material × process × lethal-fume table, hot-work fire-watch rules, OSHA 1910.252 cheat-sheet — the things you actually look up at 7 AM.",
      "materials": "Materials",
      "math_blueprint_mode": "Math + Blueprint mode",
      "measurement_tolerance": "📏 Measurement + tolerance",
      "measurement_tools_tolerance": "📏 Measurement tools + tolerance",
      "memory_hooks_for_common_symbols": "Memory hooks for common symbols",
      "mental_emotional_realities_of_trade_wo": "Mental + emotional realities of trade work",
